---
/**
 * EntryPoints — where rodents get into a Pacific Northwest house.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS DRAWN RATHER THAN SOURCED. The owner sent a very good rodent
 * entry diagram from City of Berkeley Vector Control on 2 Sep 2026 and it could
 * not be used, for three reasons worth recording so nobody re-litigates it:
 *
 *   1. IT DISAGREED WITH THIS SITE. Its note put a mouse at a nickel-sized hole
 *      and a rat at a half dollar — roughly 21mm and 30mm. This site says a
 *      quarter of an inch and half an inch, on THIRTY-FOUR pages, and the
 *      quarter inch is also the Bellingham municipal code threshold the
 *      commercial and rental content leans on. A caption arguing with the
 *      paragraph beside it is worse than no picture.
 *   2. IT WAS A CALIFORNIA HOUSE. Barrel clay tile, stucco, a citrus tree
 *      dropping fruit. This site's own copy says pest information "written for
 *      the South does not describe what happens here", and puts that argument
 *      on the blog twice. Illustrating it with a Mediterranean house would have
 *      undercut the one thing these pages are for.
 *   3. LICENSING. Credited to a California municipality, whose works are not
 *      automatically public domain the way federal ones are.
 *
 * So: composition shingle, lap siding, a vented crawlspace, an attached garage
 * and a conifer with a branch on the roof. That is the housing stock this
 * company actually works on, and every callout is something in their own photo
 * library.
 *
 * SVG, NOT AN IMAGE FILE. It is a few kilobytes, stays sharp at any size and on
 * any screen, needs no width/height guessing, and — the reason that decided it
 * — it is drawn in the site's own tokens, so it re-colors itself in dark mode
 * instead of being a white rectangle. A PNG would have to be exported twice and
 * would still be wrong on the third theme somebody adds.
 *
 * THE LABELS ARE HTML, NOT SVG TEXT. Numbers sit on the drawing; the words sit
 * in an ordered list underneath. Text inside SVG is not reliably selectable, is
 * skipped by some translation tools, and would not wrap on a phone. This way
 * the list is real content, the numbers tie it to the picture, and the whole
 * block still makes sense read aloud in order.
 *
 * NO COLORS HERE. Every fill and stroke is a class painted in §21d of
 * src/styles/global.css, which is the rule for every component on this site.
 */
import { business } from '../data/business';

interface Props {
  /** Heading level context differs between the two pages that carry this. */
  heading?: string;
}
const { heading = 'Where they actually get in' } = Astro.props;

/* The callouts, in the order a technician would work a building: roofline
   down to the ground, then the harborage that feeds it. Numbers here are the
   numbers drawn on the SVG — change one and change both. */
const points = [
  { n: 1,  t: 'Soffit and roofline gaps',      d: 'Where the roof meets the wall, and where two roof planes meet. Roof rats work the top half of a building and this is the way in.' },
  { n: 2,  t: 'Gable and attic vents',         d: 'A louvered vent with no screen behind it is an open door. Screens rust, bend outward and are rarely looked at.' },
  { n: 3,  t: 'Chimney and flashing',          d: 'The gap where a chimney passes the roofline, and lifted or torn flashing around it.' },
  { n: 4,  t: 'Branches touching the roof',    d: 'A limb over the roof is a bridge. Cutting it back is usually cheaper than anything done afterwards.' },
  { n: 5,  t: 'Gutters and downspouts',        d: 'The bracket line gives a climbing route, and a downspout against siding hides the gap behind it.' },
  { n: 6,  t: 'Utility penetrations',          d: 'Water and waste pipes, electrical, cable, and the dryer vent. Drilled oversize, sealed with foam if at all.' },
  { n: 7,  t: 'Where siding meets foundation', d: 'The single most common entry we find. A continuous line around the whole building, and nobody looks at it.' },
  { n: 8,  t: 'Crawlspace vents',              d: 'Screens corrode from the ground side. Weeds growing across a vent are why one goes unchecked for years.' },
  { n: 9,  t: 'Crawlspace access hatch',       d: 'Often a board leaning against an opening. It keeps the weather out and nothing else.' },
  { n: 10, t: 'Garage door corners',           d: 'The bottom corners, where the seal lifts away from the jamb. Visible as daylight from inside with the door shut.' },
  { n: 11, t: 'Door thresholds',               d: 'A worn sweep under an exterior door. Check it the same way — from inside, in daylight.' },
  { n: 12, t: 'Woodpiles and vegetation',      d: 'Not an entry point but the reason there is pressure on one. Cover against the wall is harborage, and it feeds everything above.' },
];
---
<section class="entrypoints">
  <h2>{heading}</h2>

  <figure>
    <div class="ep-frame">
      {/* role="img" plus a title and desc: a screen reader announces one
          picture with a sentence, rather than reading out sixty path
          elements. The numbered list below carries the detail. */}
      {/* viewBox cropped to the drawing rather than left at a round 900x540.
          The original wasted about a quarter of the height on empty sky, which
          on a phone is a quarter of the screen spent on nothing and makes every
          callout number a quarter smaller than it needs to be. */}
      <svg
        viewBox="15 100 870 435"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="ep-title ep-desc"
        class="ep-svg"
      >
        <title id="ep-title">Rodent entry points on a Pacific Northwest house</title>
        <desc id="ep-desc">
          A line drawing of a single-story house with a composition shingle
          gable roof, lap siding, an attached garage and a vented crawlspace,
          with twelve numbered markers on the places rodents commonly enter.
          Each number is described in the list below the drawing.
        </desc>

        {/* ---- ground ---- */}
        <line class="ep-ground" x1="20" y1="470" x2="880" y2="470" />

        {/* ---- conifer, because this is not California ---- */}
        <g class="ep-tree">
          <path class="ep-tree-fill" d="M690 470 L690 300 M640 320 L690 250 L740 320 Z M650 260 L690 200 L730 260 Z M660 210 L690 165 L720 210 Z" />
          <path class="ep-branch" d="M655 258 C 610 250 570 232 540 216" />
        </g>

        {/* ---- main house body ---- */}
        <rect class="ep-wall" x="180" y="215" width="330" height="255" />

        {/* lap siding lines */}
        <g class="ep-siding">
          <line x1="180" y1="245" x2="510" y2="245" /><line x1="180" y1="275" x2="510" y2="275" />
          <line x1="180" y1="305" x2="510" y2="305" /><line x1="180" y1="335" x2="510" y2="335" />
          <line x1="180" y1="365" x2="510" y2="365" /><line x1="180" y1="395" x2="510" y2="395" />
        </g>

        {/* gable roof + soffit overhang */}
        <path class="ep-roof" d="M160 222 L345 118 L530 222 Z" />
        <line class="ep-line" x1="160" y1="222" x2="530" y2="222" />

        {/* gable vent */}
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="325" y="160" width="40" height="26" rx="3" />
        <g class="ep-siding">
          <line x1="325" y1="167" x2="365" y2="167" /><line x1="325" y1="174" x2="365" y2="174" />
          <line x1="325" y1="181" x2="365" y2="181" />
        </g>

        {/* chimney */}
        <rect class="ep-wall" x="415" y="130" width="34" height="62" />
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="411" y="124" width="42" height="10" />

        {/* front door + threshold */}
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="228" y="360" width="48" height="110" />
        <line class="ep-line-strong" x1="228" y1="466" x2="276" y2="466" />

        {/* window */}
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="330" y="300" width="70" height="52" />
        <line class="ep-line" x1="365" y1="300" x2="365" y2="352" />

        {/* downspout */}
        <line class="ep-pipe" x1="500" y1="222" x2="500" y2="440" />
        <line class="ep-pipe" x1="500" y1="440" x2="486" y2="452" />

        {/* ---- foundation band + crawlspace vents ---- */}
        <rect class="ep-foundation" x="180" y="428" width="580" height="42" />
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="300" y="438" width="34" height="20" rx="2" />
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="430" y="438" width="34" height="20" rx="2" />
        {/* access hatch */}
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="196" y="434" width="40" height="28" rx="2" />

        {/* ---- attached garage ---- */}
        <rect class="ep-wall" x="510" y="290" width="250" height="180" />
        <path class="ep-roof" d="M494 296 L635 232 L776 296 Z" />
        <line class="ep-line" x1="494" y1="296" x2="776" y2="296" />
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="545" y="345" width="185" height="125" />
        <g class="ep-siding">
          <line x1="545" y1="375" x2="730" y2="375" /><line x1="545" y1="405" x2="730" y2="405" />
          <line x1="545" y1="435" x2="730" y2="435" />
        </g>

        {/* utility penetrations on the garage wall */}
        <circle class="ep-detail" cx="748" cy="392" r="7" />
        <rect class="ep-detail" x="740" y="415" width="16" height="12" rx="2" />

        {/* ---- woodpile ---- */}
        <g class="ep-detail">
          <circle cx="120" cy="440" r="9" /><circle cx="140" cy="440" r="9" />
          <circle cx="160" cy="440" r="9" /><circle cx="130" cy="424" r="9" />
          <circle cx="150" cy="424" r="9" /><circle cx="140" cy="408" r="9" />
        </g>

        {/* ---- numbered callouts ---- */}
        <g class="ep-marks">
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="196" y1="196" x2="176" y2="224" /><circle cx="196" cy="188" r="14" /><text x="196" y="188">1</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="345" y1="142" x2="345" y2="158" /><circle cx="345" cy="128" r="14" /><text x="345" y="128">2</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="470" y1="140" x2="450" y2="150" /><circle cx="482" cy="134" r="14" /><text x="482" y="134">3</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="596" y1="212" x2="566" y2="224" /><circle cx="608" cy="206" r="14" /><text x="608" y="206">4</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="524" y1="262" x2="504" y2="278" /><circle cx="534" cy="254" r="14" /><text x="534" y="254">5</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="800" y1="392" x2="762" y2="396" /><circle cx="814" cy="390" r="14" /><text x="814" y="390">6</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="392" y1="410" x2="392" y2="428" /><circle cx="392" cy="400" r="14" /><text x="392" y="400">7</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="317" y1="490" x2="317" y2="462" /><circle cx="317" cy="504" r="14" /><text x="317" y="504">8</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="216" y1="490" x2="216" y2="464" /><circle cx="216" cy="504" r="14" /><text x="216" y="504">9</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="560" y1="490" x2="556" y2="470" /><circle cx="560" cy="504" r="14" /><text x="560" y="504">10</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="252" y1="342" x2="252" y2="358" /><circle cx="252" cy="332" r="14" /><text x="252" y="332">11</text></g>
          <g><line class="ep-leader" x1="96" y1="418" x2="118" y2="428" /><circle cx="86" cy="412" r="14" /><text x="86" y="412">12</text></g>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      Drawn for the housing stock here — composition shingle, lap siding, a
      vented crawlspace and an attached garage. A mouse needs about a quarter of
      an inch and a rat about half an inch, which is why most of what matters on
      this drawing is a gap nobody would call a hole.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <ol class="ep-list">
    {points.map((p) => (
      <li>
        <b>{p.t}</b> {p.d}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <p class="ep-note">
    Every one of these is something we seal. If you want it looked at rather
    than guessed at, call or text{' '}
    <a href={`tel:${business.phoneE164}`}>{business.phone}</a> — or read how the
    work is done on the{' '}
    <a href="/services/exclusion-and-repairs/">exclusion and repairs</a> page.
  </p>
</section>
